// Polkit rule for choosing a Wi-Fi network from the management UIs (issue #194).
//
// A parent whose device has no network reaches for the companion app over BLE
// -- the one management transport that still works when there is nothing to
// route -- picks an SSID and types a key. Writing the profile that results
// needs `org.freedesktop.NetworkManager.settings.modify.system`, and joining
// it needs `org.freedesktop.NetworkManager.network-control` as well.
//
// The second is easy to miss, and was: polkit grants `network-control` to any
// active local session, so every measurement taken from a logged-in shell
// passed without it. The custodian has no session. Without the second action
// NetworkManager refuses `AddAndActivateConnection2` with "Not authorized to
// control networking" -- so a parent could save a network for later but never
// join one, which is the one thing a device with no network needs.
//
// Ubuntu's own rule grants that action to local, active members of `sudo` or
// `netdev`. The kiosk user is in neither, and must stay in neither, because
// **every activity runs as the kiosk user**: a grant to that uid is a grant to
// every game and browser a child can start.
//
// That is not a theoretical worry. It was measured on NetworkManager 1.54.3:
// with a temporary rule granting nothing but `settings.modify.system`,
// `GetSecrets` returned the stored passphrase for every saved network. There
// is no separate polkit action for reading secrets -- `pkaction` lists none --
// so the write grant *is* the read grant. A child with a shell on the host
// would learn the house WiFi key, and an activity inside the kiosk's graphical
// session satisfies `subject.local && subject.active` just as the launcher
// does. See
// `docs/ai/history/2026-09-21 004 wifi-against-real-networkmanager (#194).md`.
//
// So the grant goes to the state custodian, `lunchbox-stated`, running as
// `lunchbox-state` -- a uid nothing inside the session can reach: not the
// directory, not the socket, not the process. `lunchboxd` asks it, over a
// socket it accepts only from the kiosk session's own cgroup.
//
// Install path: /etc/polkit-1/rules.d/50-lunchbox-network.rules
//
// ## What this widens, said plainly
//
// polkit cannot narrow this action to wireless profiles, so the rule really
// grants `lunchbox-state` the right to change ANY NetworkManager setting on
// this machine -- a VPN, the DNS servers, an ethernet profile -- and, per the
// measurement above, to read back every stored network secret.
// `network-control` adds the right to bring any connection up or down, which
// is strictly less than rewriting it, and changes nothing about that sentence.
//
// What keeps that acceptable is the shape of the daemon holding it, not the
// shape of the rule. The custodian has no network of its own
// (`PrivateNetwork=yes`, `RestrictAddressFamilies=AF_UNIX`), no capabilities,
// spawns nothing, and answers a protocol whose wireless requests are a typed
// join request and two UUIDs. It **never accepts a NetworkManager settings
// dictionary over the socket**, so there is no way to ask it to write a
// setting it was not written to write. That typed boundary is the real
// permission; this rule is only what lets it act.
//
// It reads no secrets either: nothing in Lunchbox calls `GetSecrets`, because
// no management UI ever shows a saved password. They are write-only from every
// direction a parent or a child can reach.
//
// Without this rule the device still boots, still joins networks it already
// knows, and still shows what is in range. What stops working is saving a new
// one. The custodian checks for the rule at startup and `lunchboxd` raises the
// `Warning` diagnostic `wifi_config_unavailable`, which names this file --
// because a form that accepts a password and silently drops it is worse than
// one that says up front it cannot help.

polkit.addRule(function(action, subject) {
    if ((action.id === "org.freedesktop.NetworkManager.settings.modify.system" ||
         action.id === "org.freedesktop.NetworkManager.network-control") &&
        subject.user === "lunchbox-state") {
        return polkit.Result.YES;
    }
});
